tara: Heads up: the quickstart model-upload flow assumes SDK returns a long-running operation with `wait=true` semantics like the REST example. Node SDK currently returns a promise that resolves only after polling completes internally and it doesn't expose `operation.id` or `cancel()` so the sampleapp can't show progress or let users cancel.

mike: Hmm. Which sampleapp flow is this?

tara: The 'local-models' quickstart. README shows curl: ```curl -X POST /models:upload -d '{"file": "...", "wait": false}' ``` then polls `GET /operations/{op_id}` to stream progress and optionally `POST /operations/{op_id}:cancel`. The SDK has `client.models.upload(file)` that blocks and returns final model metadata.

sophia: That's a parity gap. The REST route intentionally supports fire-and-poll because uploads can take minutes and we want the UI to show progress and let users cancel. Blocking calls in SDK break the UX and make CI hang.

mike: Why did we implement it that way? IIRC early SDK design hid polling for simplicity. We added helpers for synchronous convenience but didn't expose operation objects.

sophia: Can we add a thin `operations` surface to the SDK with `startUpload(..., { wait: boolean })` returning `{ operationId }` when wait=false, plus a `operations.get(id)` and `operations.cancel(id)`? That would mirror REST.

tara: Also need streaming progress events. REST `GET /operations/{id}` returns `progress` field and `status` (RUNNING/FAILED/DONE). Sampleapp wants to render incremental progress bars without polling delays. Maybe add `operations.watch(id)` that yields events (or an async iterator) for Node.

mike: Agreed. Implementation plan: 1) change SDK upload to accept option `wait: true|false` default true (backwards compat). 2) when wait=false, return { operationId } immediately. 3) add `client.operations.get(operationId)` and `client.operations.cancel(operationId)`. 4) add `client.operations.watch(operationId)` using eventsource or polling; document caveats.

sophia: Tests: add unit tests verifying that wait=false doesn't block and that returned operationId can be polled to DONE. Add an integration smoke test in quickstart CI that uses watch() to render progress and cancels mid-way to ensure cancel flow works.

tara: I'm going to patch the quickstart UI to use a REST fallback for now: call `/models:upload?wait=false` then poll `/operations/{id}` until DONE. Sample snippet:```js
const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}/models:upload?wait=false`, { method: 'POST', headers: authHeader, body: formData });
const { operationId } = await res.json();
let info;
while (true) {
  info = await (await fetch(`${API_BASE}/operations/${operationId}`, { headers: authHeader })).json();
  showProgress(info.progress);
  if (info.status !== 'RUNNING') break;
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));
}
```

mike: I'll take the SDK work. Will add watch() with a fallback to short-polling if EventSource isn't available. Also expose cancel(). We'll keep existing `models.upload()` but deprecate blocking behavior in the next major.

sophia: UX note: update quickstart docs to recommend non-blocking flow for uploads and demo cancel. Add a short blurb in SDK changelog about operations surface parity.

devex-bot: Created issue #594: "Node SDK: operations parity - non-blocking uploads, watch(), and cancel()" assigned to @mike. https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/594

tara: Thanks. Deploying the sampleapp REST fallback so live demos don't hang. I'll remove once SDK PR lands and quickstart CI verifies it.

mike: PR incoming with operations surface + tests. Ping @sam for review once draft is up.

sam: Great. Also add a small DX checklist item: any REST route that returns an operation id should have parity helpers in SDK before docs reference them.
